WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.619
Your service calls a card issuer through a load balancer, which hands each request to the next healthy backend.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.644
Each answers in about 120 milliseconds.

3
00:00:12.644 --> 00:00:17.237
Then backend 2 slows down, and a request sent there just waits.

4
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:24.046
So every call gets a timeout, a bit above the issuer's normal slowest answer: 500 milliseconds.

5
00:00:24.046 --> 00:00:27.824
Then the client retries, and lands on another backend.

6
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:30.662
That's safe because this call only reads.

7
00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:35.791
Retrying a payment needs an idempotency key, or the card could be charged twice.

8
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.353
Now the issuer restarts, and is down for 250 milliseconds.

9
00:00:40.353 --> 00:00:42.755
A thousand clients fail at once.

10
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:51.272
Each retries after a fixed 100 milliseconds, so all thousand retries land together, where the issuer can answer 100.

11
00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:54.999
Backoff doubles the wait: 100, 200, 400, 800.

12
00:00:54.999 --> 00:01:02.369
The waves spread out, but every client counts the same way, so each wave still lands at once.

13
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:05.935
After six tries, 600 clients give up.

14
00:01:05.935 --> 00:01:10.943
The issuer saw 5,400 requests and answered 400 clients.

15
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:15.904
Jitter fixes it: each client waits a random time up to its ceiling.

16
00:01:15.904 --> 00:01:20.747
Retries spread out, and all 1,000 are answered in about 1.1 seconds.

17
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:27.542
Retries also multiply: three layers that each try three times turn one request into 27.

18
00:01:27.542 --> 00:01:31.775
A retry budget caps them at about 10 percent of traffic.

19
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:33.669
Last, the connections.

20
00:01:33.669 --> 00:01:40.321
A new one costs a TCP and a TLS round trip, so a pool keeps some open and reuses them.

21
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.778
But the balancer forgets a connection idle for 350 seconds, and tells nobody.

22
00:01:45.778 --> 00:01:48.245
The next request on it gets a reset.

23
00:01:48.245 --> 00:01:50.781
Keep the pool's idle timeout shorter.

24
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.925
Retries arrive just when the issuer is weakest.

25
00:01:53.925 --> 00:01:57.285
Spread them out at random, and cap them with a budget.
